---
id: ai-synthesis
title: "Two AI reports, one design decision"
description: "Two coding agents studied the same Git problem and recommended different paths. Reproducing their claims mattered more than choosing a winner."
status: draft
series: git-splice
seriesOrder: 5
topics: [ai, research, open-source]
---

I gave Claude and Codex the same research problem: compare my `git-subtrees` tool with `git-subrepo` and recommend a direction. Their reports agreed on much of the architecture and disagreed on the central implementation choice.

One recommended building on subrepo's engine. The other recommended borrowing its ideas without adopting the engine. Reading both reports did not resolve the disagreement. Running their claims did.

## Agreement was not the decision

Both analyses recognized the same broad facts:

- committed synchronization state enables incremental work;
- a remote for one folder is the wrong abstraction;
- pulls should produce one ordinary monorepo commit;
- pushes need rebuilt folder history;
- conflicts belong in the normal working tree.

This overlap was valuable. It showed that the design space had narrowed. But agreement between models is evidence of consistency, not evidence of correctness. The shared assumptions still needed tests.

## Reproduction changed the recommendation

The decisive experiment used the workflow I rely on: perform a pull on a feature branch, squash-merge it, then push from `main`.

Subrepo failed because its stored `parent` commit no longer existed. My own tool also failed, differently, because its history-encoded synchronization commit disappeared. These were not theoretical edge cases. Squash merging is the default operation in my repositories.

That result made “reuse the engine” much less attractive. Fixing the unstable parent, deterministic commit metadata, branch following, status, and diff would remove much of what the engine provided. Borrowing the architectural ideas became the smaller choice.

## Synthesis is a separate task

Using multiple agents did not give me a vote to count. It gave me competing models to interrogate. The useful workflow was:

1. ask both to make concrete claims;
2. list agreements and disagreements explicitly;
3. turn disputed claims into reproducible scenarios;
4. keep ideas whose constraints matched the real workflow;
5. record why rejected recommendations were rejected.

The synthesis document became more valuable than either original report because it connected recommendations to decisions and decisions to evidence.

## Naming came after the model

Once `git subtree` had disappeared from the production design, the old name `git-subtrees` became misleading. I explored names around embedding, layering, and pairing. The model eventually suggested **splice**: a repository is spliced into a path, while its history can be spliced back onto upstream.

The state file becomes `.splice`; the command becomes `git splice`. Commands that modify repositories require an explicit path or `--all`, while observational commands cover every splice by default.

The name was not a branding exercise added at the end. It tested whether the model could be explained consistently.

My main lesson from this research was not “use two AIs.” It was to make disagreement productive. Ask for falsifiable claims, reproduce the ones that affect the design, and keep human ownership of the constraints. Agents accelerated the search. The workflow—and the decision—remained mine.
